WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.120
As the Fast franchise hurtles&nbsp;
toward the finish line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.060
not every driver who starred in&nbsp;
the franchise has stayed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.320
race. So what happened to the actress who&nbsp;
played Suki in "2 Fast 2 Furious," anyway?

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.240
Looking back on the "Fast &amp; Furious" franchise, "2&nbsp;
Fast 2 Furious" set the tone for what was coming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.880
The Vin-Diesel-less sequel saw Brian taking&nbsp;
down an Argentinian drug lord with a racecar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.560
something that the modern "Fast" movies are&nbsp;
all too familiar with. It also introduced a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.440
franchise veterans, with Roman and Tej joining the&nbsp;
series. Suki, played by Devon Aoki, also played a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.640
memorable role in "2 Fast 2 Furious," only to&nbsp;
disappear from the franchise without a trace.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.800
Devon Aoki is best known as a model, finding&nbsp;
massive success in the industry before hitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.600
adulthood. However, in the early 2000s,&nbsp;
Aoki decided to test out the movie industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.040
appearing in a few films before 2010.&nbsp;
Besides "2 Fast 2 Furious," she appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:55.320
in movies like "Sin City," "D.E.B.S.," and the&nbsp;
infamous "Dead or Alive." However, in 2009,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.820
she retired from acting to focus on raising&nbsp;
her kids, which might explain why she's never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.740
returned to the "Fast &amp; Furious" franchise. Since&nbsp;
then, she's only appeared in two documentaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.720
with one of them, "I'll Sleep When I'm Dead,"&nbsp;
focusing on her half-brother DJ Steve Aoki.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.140
In recent years, however, Aoki has&nbsp;
returned to the fashion industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.360
which leads us to ask: could we see her return&nbsp;
to "Fast &amp; Furious?" The franchise has brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.560
back old characters time and time again,&nbsp;
so it's arguably about time Suki returns.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.200
"Alright, ladies. We're good. We're good."

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.180
"Fast X" is the beginning of the end for the "Fast&nbsp;
and Furious" franchise, so time's running out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.860
for Aoki's Suki to return in the main series&nbsp;
if she ever does. The next installments will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:38.640
likely celebrate the entire series, so the&nbsp;
more characters that come back, the better.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:42.060
A spin-off may be the next best&nbsp;
option if fans don't get Suki&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.060
back in time for the franchise's&nbsp;
conclusion. In a 2022 interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.540
Universal Pictures chairwoman Donna&nbsp;
Langley told Business Insider,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:52.140
"I would love to see a female 'Fast.'&nbsp;
So would Vin [Diesel]. We have so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:56.220
great and amazing female characters in our&nbsp;
franchise and now there's the new addition of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.120
Brie Larson to add to the incredible roster.&nbsp;
I would love to see us do a female 'Fast.'"

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.440
If that's the case, what better female&nbsp;
character to include than Suki? "2 Fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.980
2 Furious" introduces her as the leader&nbsp;
of an all-female street racing crew,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.480
and she shows that she has what it takes to&nbsp;
race against anybody. If Universal plans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.280
do a female "Fast" movie, Suki could be in it,&nbsp;
assuming that Aoki is willing to return to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.840
screen. Pairing her with some of the other women&nbsp;
of the franchise sounds like a recipe for success.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:27.480
It's not impossible. Devon Aoki appears to have&nbsp;
retired from regular acting for now, but she has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.980
graced a few music videos since she stopped&nbsp;
appearing in movies and TV shows on a regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:37.140
basis. In 2013, she had a cameo in the video for&nbsp;
The Killers' single "Just Another Girl," which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.040
shows her hanging out in a makeshift theater,&nbsp;
watching a black-and-white film on the screen.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.120
Elsewhere, Aoki was one of a number of&nbsp;
celebrities who appeared in the video for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.220
Fergie's track "M.I.L.F.$." In this one, the model&nbsp;
joined a slew of other celebrities to portray a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.780
group of moms in a bubblegum-esque suburban&nbsp;
neighborhood. Aoki shows up in a few scenes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:58.500
rocking fancy outfits and hanging out&nbsp;
with Fergie's star-studded entourage.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.420
The most recent music video Aoki starred in&nbsp;
was for Steve Aoki and BTS' "Waste It On Me"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:07.980
collaboration. The video sees her out on&nbsp;
a date with a member of BTS, and she also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:12.240
gets to mime some of the song's lyrics into&nbsp;
a microphone throughout the video. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.260
a few music videos and documentary&nbsp;
appearances don't necessarily imply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.740
that Aoki is dying to return to the&nbsp;
now-massive franchise she once made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.940
a small appearance in. But if the franchise&nbsp;
does want to dig deep into its history again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.840
the records have shown that it's hard&nbsp;
to say no when Vin Diesel comes calling.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.311
"Dom, don't get yourself killed,&nbsp;
okay? You're my favorite American."

